  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nShowers and storms have exited to the south and east, with partly to\nmostly cloudy sky and northerly winds bringing a gradual drop in \ndewpoints. Dry conditions today into tonight, along with gusty \nnorthwest wind and a noticeable reduction in humidity. Under a \nbuilding surface ridge, highs through the weekend are expected to run\nmainly in the low to mid 80s with lows in the 60s, and dew points \nsettling into the mid 60s or lower, yielding more comfortable \nconditions compared to recent heat and humidity.\n\nFrom Sunday into early next week, the ridge axis shifts east,\nallowing low-level southerly flow to slowly reestablish across the\ncentral Plains and support a gradual warm-up. Highs should trend \ntoward the upper 80s to low 90s by Monday and Tuesday, with slowly \nincreasing dew points but generally dry conditions persisting.\n\nBy mid-week, ensemble and deterministic guidance indicate another\nshortwave trough translating through the northern and central Plains\nwith a trailing surface front moving through the area, supporting a \nreturn of shower and thunderstorm chances. This far out though there\nremains considerable uncertainty on timing, precipitation amounts \nand potential strength of storms, but it remains the most likely \nperiod over the next seven days of seeing rain.",
